# Part 2 — Sleeping Stylist with Monitor

## Group Members
- Zhen Keat Chua
- Zi Dane Yan

## How to Compile and Run

```bash
make
./sleepingStylistMon
```

## What to Observe

### Signal-and-Continue Discipline
The key proof is in the output. After a thread is woken by `cv_signal`, it prints:

```
[CV] Thread resumed after cv_wait — re-entered monitor (signal-and-continue)
```

This line appears AFTER the signaler has already continued running and released
the monitor — NOT immediately when the signal is sent. This confirms signal-and-continue:
the signaler keeps going, and the woken thread must re-compete for the monitor lock.

If this were signal-and-wait, the signaler would have paused and the woken thread
would have run immediately inside the monitor. That does NOT happen here.

### Salon Empty (Stylist Sleeping)
Look for:
```
[STYLIST] Salon empty. Stylist going to sleep. (Empty count: X)
```
Each occurrence increments the "Salon empty" counter visible in mon_debugPrint output.

### Salon Full
Look for:
```
[CUSTOMER X] Salon FULL (6/6 chairs). Will try again. (Full count: X)
```

### Debug Print Consistency
The chair grid printed by mon_debugPrint:
```
| 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | => 3
```
The number of 1s should always match the `=> N` count. This confirms the
chair array and customerCount variable stay in sync.

### No Starvation
All 75 customers will eventually print:
```
[CUSTOMER X] Done. Leaving salon.
```

